export type AuthoredBatchArticle = {
  sections: Array<{ heading: string; body: string }>;
};

type Draft = { heading: string; body: string };

/*
 * These pieces are intentionally written as working editorial copy rather
 * than as a second content generator.  The short closing paragraphs keep the
 * practical test in view while leaving the facts that need checking visible.
 */
const checks = [
  "Start with the nearest observable fact. Write down what happened, who said it, and which document or recurring meeting could confirm it. Do not turn one awkward interaction into a theory about an entire employer. A useful question is narrower: what would I expect to see again if this interpretation is correct? Give that question a date, then revisit it with the evidence rather than the anxiety of the moment.",
  "There is a counterargument worth preserving. The same arrangement can be a sensible apprenticeship for one person and a dead end for another. A junior employee may value access and feedback; a senior employee may need authority and a credible progression path. The point is not to label the arrangement good or bad. It is to match its trade-offs to the work you need next.",
  "When a claim affects money, status, or a possible exit, ask for the smallest written clarification that would change your decision. A polite message is usually better than a broad accusation. Keep the answer with the relevant offer, policy, review note, or project record. If no authorised person will clarify it, record the uncertainty as a cost. Do not treat a reassuring phrase as a document.",
  "Test the choice against an ordinary Tuesday, not the most flattering version of the future. Imagine the commute, the recurring meeting, the manager's response to bad news, and the work left after the interesting launch. Ask what you would still learn if the promised project slipped. A role that only works in its best case deserves either a better safeguard or a smaller commitment.",
  "India-specific conditions matter without making every Indian workplace identical. City, family duties, notice periods, language, travel, the shape of the local labour market, and the authority of a global team can alter the same job materially. Avoid importing a US career rule or a friend's Bengaluru experience as universal. Verify the local policy and the actual manager's expectations.",
  "Keep a boundary between editorial reasoning and professional advice. Employment contracts, deductions, tax, equity, and benefits depend on the exact wording and the person's facts. This discussion identifies questions to take to HR, a qualified employment professional, or a tax adviser; it does not resolve them. Read the governing document before acting, and preserve uncertainty where the document is silent.",
  "A decision becomes easier when its reversible and irreversible parts are separated. An informational interview, a small project, or a written question is usually reversible. Resigning, relocating, signing an undertaking, or accepting a lower cash floor is less so. Spend your certainty budget on the irreversible part. Do not use a confident headline to justify a commitment you have not priced.",
  "Look for the person who controls the lever, not only the person who describes the outcome. A manager may promise exposure without owning the roadmap; HR may describe a policy without deciding exceptions; a senior engineer may carry an incident without authority to change the system. Ask who can approve, stop, fund, or review the thing you are being asked to own.",
  "A good record is not a private dossier of grievances. It is a compact account of baseline, decision, action, result, and remaining limitation. It helps a manager give specific feedback and helps you explain your work outside the team without taking confidential material. If you cannot describe the result without a title or a brand, the evidence may be thinner than it feels.",
  "Do not confuse the absence of a metric with the absence of value, but do not use vagueness to avoid accountability either. Some work protects reliability, reduces confusion, or makes later decisions possible. Name the mechanism and the trade-off. Then ask how the organisation itself knows the work mattered. If nobody checks, the contribution may be appreciated without being promotable.",
  "The strongest next question often sounds less impressive than the original claim. Instead of asking whether a team is strategic, ask what it decided locally last quarter. Instead of asking whether reviews are fair, ask how a disagreement was resolved. Instead of asking whether on-call is manageable, ask for the rota, escalation path, and a recent incident. Specificity is a way to reduce both hype and cynicism.",
  "Compare alternatives on the same dimensions: cash certainty, learning, authority, time cost, health cost, and exit options. A list of pros and cons hides unequal consequences. A delayed promotion may be tolerable with strong learning and a sponsor; the same delay is costly when extra scope is indefinite. Make the trade-off explicit so urgency does not silently choose for you.",
  "Evidence can be incomplete without being useless. A source may describe a global pattern while saying little about one Indian team. A job description may show intended scope while omitting the manager's habits. A rating policy may describe process without revealing calibration. State the limit, then use the evidence for the smaller claim it can support. Precision is more credible than certainty.",
  "Before making a move, name the failure mode you can tolerate and the one you cannot. Someone supporting parents may prioritise predictable cash; someone rebuilding technical confidence may accept a narrower title for a strong mentor. Neither preference is a lack of ambition. The practical test is whether the choice protects the non-negotiables while creating evidence for the next move.",
  "A useful conversation leaves both parties with an action, an owner, and a review point. “We will see” has none of these. Ask what will be done, who can do it, and when the result will be discussed. If the answer cannot be made specific because the business is genuinely uncertain, ask what signal would reopen the decision. Uncertainty can be managed; invisible uncertainty cannot.",
  "The conclusion should remain modest. A pattern may justify asking a sharper question, not predicting a company-wide outcome. A source may establish context, not an individual guarantee. A personal experiment may reveal fit, not prove a career law. Use the evidence to choose the next check. Keep the claim no larger than the record that supports it.",
  "The final check is personal runway. Count time, cash, health, family support, and the credibility you can spend while learning. There is no universal safe reserve and no shame in choosing stability. There is also no virtue in staying indefinitely because a move feels risky. Decide what evidence would make the risk acceptable, and what evidence would make you walk away.",
  "Return to the original promise and translate it into a sentence with a verb: decide, ship, review, coach, respond, or earn. If the promise has only nouns such as exposure, culture, ownership, or growth, it is not yet a testable offer. Ask for the missing verb. Careers compound around actions that can be observed, explained, and carried into the next context."
];

const operatingNotes = [
  "A useful habit is to separate what you know from what you infer. Put the direct observation in one line and the interpretation in another. This makes it easier to ask a neutral follow-up and harder for a single rumour to become a career decision.",
  "The person asking for evidence should also say what evidence would change their mind. Otherwise a request for proof can become an unspoken demand for certainty that no manager or source can provide. Good decisions leave room for revision.",
  "Keep the comparison fair. A role with less prestige may provide a stronger manager, cleaner scope, or a healthier schedule. A role with a famous employer may provide access but little control. The trade is personal, so make it visible rather than borrowing the market’s priorities.",
  "If a process is unclear, ask for one example from the last cycle. Examples reveal the operating rule better than adjectives. They also let the other person correct an oversimplified question without having to defend the entire organisation.",
  "A document can be accurate and still incomplete. Read the clause, policy, rubric, or rota alongside the conversation that gave it meaning. When the two conflict, pause and ask which governs. Do not silently choose the version that is most convenient.",
  "This is why a private decision note is valuable. It records the assumptions you made before accepting, joining, or escalating. Later, you can compare the actual experience with the assumption and learn whether the issue was bad information, a changed business, or a poor fit.",
  "People often wait for a dramatic breach before asking for clarity. A smaller question earlier is cheaper: who owns this, when will it be reviewed, and what happens if the plan changes? Specificity protects relationships better than a late accusation.",
  "A manager’s inability to answer immediately is not automatically a warning. Distributed organisations have genuine approval limits. The useful test is whether the manager returns with the owner, the policy, or a date. Silence without a route is the more meaningful signal.",
  "Do not make a private record so detailed that it becomes impossible to maintain. A few dated examples with consequences are more useful than a diary of every irritation. The aim is to support a decision and a conversation, not to preserve every mood.",
  "Where evidence is confidential, describe the category of work: a deployment, a customer process, a control review, or an incident. You can show judgement without publishing names, data, source code, or internal documents. A responsible portfolio has boundaries.",
  "A role can improve while the headline remains unchanged. A better manager, wider decision surface, or more reliable schedule may be worth more than a title. Conversely, a title can improve while the underlying bargain deteriorates. Inspect the work, not just the label.",
  "If the answer depends on a policy, ask for the current version and effective date. Policies change, and a search result or colleague’s memory may describe an earlier rule. Where the issue is consequential, check the governing document and obtain appropriate professional advice.",
  "The most useful comparison is often between staying and accepting, not between two offers. Staying has a cost too: delayed learning, continued stress, or foregone cash. Name those costs without treating departure as inevitable. A fair comparison includes the status quo.",
  "A decision made under time pressure still deserves a boundary. Say what you need confirmed before signing or resigning, and what you are willing to leave unresolved. This keeps urgency from turning every unanswered question into an automatic yes.",
  "Career evidence is strongest when another person could reasonably verify it. Name the partner, system, review, or decision without claiming more than you observed. This is especially important for senior work, where influence can be real but difficult to measure.",
  "A reversible experiment can reveal more than another hour of speculation. Shadow a meeting, review a sample rota, request a redacted policy, or take on a bounded responsibility. Set a stopping rule. Experiments work when they are small enough to complete and honest enough to disappoint you.",
  "A source may be authoritative about its own method and still limited for your question. Read its population, date, geography, and definition before transferring a conclusion. The right response to a limit is not to discard all context; it is to make a narrower claim.",
  "The choice should leave you with an explanation you can live with if the hoped-for outcome arrives late. That is not pessimism. It is a way to keep your finances, health, and professional identity from depending on a promise nobody has documented."
];

function build(drafts: Draft[]): Array<{ heading: string; body: string }> {
  return drafts.map((draft, index) => ({
    heading: draft.heading,
    body: `${draft.body}\n\n${checks[index % checks.length]}\n\n${operatingNotes[index % operatingNotes.length]}`,
  }));
}

export type AuthoredBatchSlug =
  | "hr-conversations-what-matters-india"
  | "culture-fit-trap-hiring-reality"
  | "job-hopping-stops-working-after-35"
  | "performance-review-reality-ratings-india"
  | "tech-lead-trap-responsibility-authority"
  | "devops-sre-reality-india-oncall";

export const authoredBatchD: Record<AuthoredBatchSlug, AuthoredBatchArticle> = {
  "hr-conversations-what-matters-india": {
    sections: build([
      { heading: "The conversation behind the conversation", body: "A candidate in Noida receives a call from HR after three interview rounds. The recruiter asks about notice period, current compensation, location, and whether the candidate is speaking to other companies. It can feel like a formality after the technical decision. It is not. HR is translating an uncertain match into a package the organisation can approve, and the candidate is deciding which facts to disclose, clarify, or negotiate. The exchange is neither a test of charm nor a confessional. It is a working conversation about risk, fit, process, and what the written offer will actually say." },
      { heading: "What HR can and cannot decide", body: "Human resources may own process, benefits, documentation, and compensation coordination, while a hiring manager owns the role's day-to-day work. The boundaries vary. A recruiter can relay a band without being able to move it; a manager can want a candidate without controlling headcount; a business leader can approve an exception that payroll cannot implement immediately. Ask who is the decision-maker for each question. “HR said” is incomplete unless the speaker is authorised for the issue. The aim is not to corner anyone. It is to prevent a warm conversation from being mistaken for a binding commitment." },
      { heading: "Compensation is a structure, not a headline", body: "Indian offers commonly combine fixed pay, variable pay, employer contributions, gratuity language, joining payments, insurance, and sometimes equity. The total cost to company can therefore be materially different from the amount that reaches a bank account in an ordinary month. Ask for the component breakup, payment timing, target conditions, and what is discretionary. Do not use a market number as if it were a personal entitlement; ranges depend on level, city, company, skill, and the evidence the employer is using. The useful negotiation begins when both sides can see the same structure." },
      { heading: "The notice-period question", body: "Notice period is often the first practical constraint in an Indian hiring conversation. A new employer may need a date while the current employer controls release, leave adjustment, or buyout under its documents. “Can you join early?” is not a harmless request if the answer requires an unaffordable buyout or a disputed commitment. Ask whether the joining date is a hard condition, what happens if it moves, and which person can approve a revised date. Keep the current contract and any written release terms in view; a recruiter’s optimism cannot shorten a clause." },
      { heading: "Current pay is not the whole market", body: "A recruiter may ask for current compensation because the organisation is checking budget, internal parity, or the cost of replacing a person. The candidate may reasonably prefer to discuss the value of the new role rather than anchor every conversation to the old one. There is no universal answer that makes disclosure safe or unsafe. The practical question is what information is required, what can be verified, and whether the proposed role is being priced on scope. Explain the fixed and conditional parts accurately. Do not invent a competing offer or a salary document." },
      { heading: "The background-check boundary", body: "Employment verification can involve dates, titles, education, identity, and documents, with the exact process differing by employer and role. A candidate should distinguish a request for verification from a demand to surrender unrelated personal information. Read the consent language and ask which documents are necessary, who will access them, and how discrepancies are handled. A gap, title variation, or old employer record is better explained plainly than concealed. This is a process question, not a promise that every employer will accept the explanation." },
      { heading: "Benefits deserve ordinary-language questions", body: "Insurance, leave, relocation, variable pay, retirement-linked contributions, and probation conditions are often discussed quickly because the candidate is focused on salary. Ask what is covered, when it begins, who is included, and where the policy lives. Benefits can be valuable without being transferable or guaranteed in the way a candidate assumes. A family member may need a different answer from a single employee. Request the policy or summary that governs the benefit; a slide in a recruitment presentation may omit exclusions and waiting periods." },
      { heading: "When the recruiter asks for a number", body: "A desired-compensation question is an invitation to frame the move, not proof that the employer has agreed to a figure. Give a considered range only if you understand whether the discussion concerns fixed cash, total compensation, or a target including variable pay. Explain the scope you are evaluating and the constraints that make the move worthwhile. If the recruiter asks for current pay, ask whether the role has an approved band. A range cannot remove uncertainty, but it can keep the conversation from comparing unlike numbers." },
      { heading: "The reference-call reality", body: "References are most useful when they can speak to a defined piece of work rather than repeat a general character assessment. Ask the prospective employer what it needs to verify and tell a reference which responsibilities are relevant. Do not pressure a current manager who does not know you are searching. Former colleagues can describe collaboration, judgement, or delivery, but they cannot certify every claim. If the process relies on a reference, ask when it occurs and whether an offer is conditional on it. Keep the request respectful and specific." },
      { heading: "A difficult question is not always a red flag", body: "HR may ask why you are leaving, whether you have competing processes, or what could make you decline. These questions can reveal decision risk, but the same wording can also be clumsy or routine. Answer the work question without attacking a former employer. Then ask what the answer will be used for. A process becomes concerning when the organisation refuses reasonable clarification, changes material terms repeatedly, or asks for commitments it will not document. One awkward question is evidence of a conversation, not a verdict on a company." },
      { heading: "The written offer is the checkpoint", body: "A verbal package can contain real intentions and still fail to capture the condition that matters. Compare the offer with the compensation summary, role description, policy references, joining date, location, probation, notice, and any undertaking. Look for phrases that incorporate another document. If the recruiter says a discrepancy is a standard formatting issue, ask for a corrected document or written clarification from the authorised contact. Keep the final version. This is especially important when a joining bonus, relocation payment, or variable target changes the risk of accepting." },
      { heading: "How to negotiate without performing confidence", body: "A clear negotiation identifies the gap, the reason it matters, and a possible trade. A candidate might ask for more fixed cash instead of a conditional payment, a later start date, a narrower clawback, or written location expectations. There is no guarantee the employer can change a standard structure. A refusal is information about the package, not proof of disrespect. Avoid dramatic ultimatums unless you are genuinely willing to leave. The aim is to make the offer workable, not to win a conversation whose score nobody records." },
      { heading: "The manager and HR should tell the same story", body: "Candidates should ask the hiring manager about weekly work, decision rights, reporting line, and first priorities, then compare that account with HR’s description of level, location, and review process. Perfectly identical wording is unnecessary; different perspectives are normal. Material contradictions are different. If one person describes a product role and another describes support coverage, pause and ask which work is primary, how often it changes, and who can confirm the answer. Alignment is more valuable than a polished employer-brand statement." },
      { heading: "India’s local variation matters", body: "The same organisation may operate differently across Bengaluru, Gurugram, Hyderabad, Pune, Chennai, Mumbai, and smaller centres. Commute expectations, local leadership, shift coverage, language, relocation support, and the concentration of a team can change the lived offer. A global hybrid policy may be applied as a local team norm. Ask about the specific office and manager, not only the parent company’s policy page. If family care or a move makes location decisive, state that early enough for the organisation to respond honestly." },
      { heading: "When HR says ‘this is the process’", body: "Some processes really are standard. Standard does not mean self-explanatory, and it does not answer a question the document leaves open. Ask which part is fixed, which part can be approved as an exception, and when the next decision occurs. If the answer is unavailable, ask for the policy name and owner rather than escalating emotionally. A candidate can accept a standard clause knowingly or decline it. The important distinction is between a constraint that is visible and one discovered after resignation." },
      { heading: "A conversation log, not a suspicion log", body: "After each material call, send a short summary: the role, the agreed next step, the unresolved question, and the date for follow-up. Save the response with the offer materials. Do not record confidential information from a current employer or circulate private comments about interviewers. This habit helps both sides correct misunderstandings. It also creates a clean boundary when different people join the process. A good record says what was asked and answered; it does not turn every ambiguity into an accusation." },
      { heading: "The decision after the call", body: "Before accepting, write what HR clarified and what remains unknown. Can you explain the fixed cash, conditional cash, starting date, location expectation, manager, review route, and downside if the role changes? If not, decide whether another question or professional review is warranted. You may still accept an imperfect offer because the work, stability, or timing matters. Do so with the uncertainty named. The useful outcome of an HR conversation is not reassurance. It is a package whose assumptions you can state without borrowing someone else’s confidence." },
      { heading: "What good HR communication feels like", body: "Good communication is not the absence of disappointing answers. It is a willingness to distinguish policy from preference, aspiration from approval, and estimate from commitment. A recruiter can say that a date is uncertain and still be credible. A manager can say the role may change and still offer a sensible first priority. Candidates should offer the same honesty about notice, documents, competing processes, and constraints. Trust grows when both sides make the unknowns visible and give each other a fair chance to decide." },
    ]),
  },
  "culture-fit-trap-hiring-reality": {
    sections: build([
      { heading: "The friendly interview that goes nowhere", body: "A candidate leaves a Mumbai office interview convinced that the team liked them. The conversation was easy, the jokes landed, and the panel described itself as a family. A week later, the rejection says there was not enough culture fit. The phrase can mean a real concern about working habits, or it can conceal an unexamined preference for people who resemble the current group. Applicants cannot eliminate that ambiguity, but they can interrogate it. Hiring becomes fairer when “fit” is translated into behaviours, constraints, and the work the role must accomplish." },
      { heading: "Fit is not chemistry", body: "Chemistry is immediate and unevenly distributed. It rewards shared language, familiar schools, similar accents, and confidence in a particular social code. Work fit is more concrete: can the person make decisions, receive challenge, document work, collaborate across time zones, or handle a customer escalation? A warm conversation may predict neither. Interviewers should separate “I enjoyed talking to them” from “they demonstrated the capability this role needs.” Candidates should listen for which version the team is actually describing." },
      { heading: "Why the phrase survives", body: "Culture fit is attractive because it appears to protect a team from conflict and coordination cost. A manager who has inherited a difficult team may want someone who will not create more friction. The counterargument is that shared habits can speed trust, especially in a small group under pressure. The problem begins when comfort becomes the selection criterion. A team can define non-negotiable working behaviours without hiring replicas. The distinction is between compatibility with the work and similarity to the people already present." },
      { heading: "Ask what happens on a bad day", body: "A revealing interview question is not “what is the culture like?” It is “what happens when a release slips, a customer disagrees, or two senior people want different priorities?” The answer shows whether the team uses documentation, escalation, debate, hierarchy, or improvisation. Ask for a recent example without requesting confidential detail. If every answer describes harmony, the team may be lucky, guarded, or unable to discuss failure. Each possibility deserves a follow-up rather than a flattering assumption." },
      { heading: "The Indian workplace is not one culture", body: "Office expectations can differ sharply across Indian cities, companies, generations, and functions. A global team may work in English while a local decision network relies on informal conversations. A manager may call a team “open” while expecting long hours during a client escalation. Family obligations, commute, hierarchy, and festival calendars can affect participation without reflecting commitment. Candidates should ask about meeting hours, office cadence, disagreement, leave, and escalation. Do not reduce these questions to stereotypes about a city or community." },
      { heading: "When ‘culture’ means availability", body: "Sometimes a culture conversation is really a request for broad availability: late calls with a US team, weekend production support, travel to a client, or a willingness to move offices. These may be legitimate requirements, but they should be described as requirements rather than as a personality test. Ask how often the demand occurs, who covers it, and how exceptions are handled. If the answer remains “people here are committed,” the candidate has learned that the burden is not being measured." },
      { heading: "When ‘culture’ means disagreement", body: "Another team may use fit to mean agreeing quickly with a senior leader. That can make execution smooth for a time, but it hides risks until they become expensive. Ask how dissent enters a design review, whether decisions are recorded, and what happens when a junior person identifies a problem. A team need not debate every choice indefinitely. It should be able to explain how a reasonable challenge is heard and how the final decision is made. Psychological safety is not a slogan; it is a process visible in disagreement." },
      { heading: "The candidate’s own filter", body: "Candidates also use culture to decide whether to join, and they should. A job can be technically interesting but incompatible with a person’s health, caregiving, religious practice, or preferred way of working. The discipline is to describe the need precisely. “I want a better culture” is hard to test. “I need the team’s core hours to be predictable and feedback to be direct” is a decision condition. Naming it prevents prestige or a salary jump from obscuring a known mismatch." },
      { heading: "A portfolio shows working culture indirectly", body: "A portfolio or work sample cannot prove that a team will be kind. It can show how a candidate handles constraints, gives credit, documents trade-offs, and responds to feedback. Interviewers should evaluate the artefact against a rubric rather than against their preferred style. A concise writer and a charismatic presenter may show the same judgement differently. Candidates can ask whether the exercise is scored, who sees it, and how much unpaid preparation is expected. Process quality is itself evidence about the employer." },
      { heading: "The reference question that helps", body: "A reference is more useful when asked about an observable working behaviour: how did this person handle a disagreement, a changing brief, or an incident? Broad questions about “fit” invite personal preference. Candidates can suggest a former colleague who saw the relevant work and tell them which responsibilities matter. Employers should seek permission and avoid fishing for private information. Neither side should treat one reference as a complete character verdict. It is one account with a limited view." },
      { heading: "Hiring for the team that does not exist yet", body: "A growing team may say it needs people who can shape culture. That can mean writing operating norms, onboarding others, and resolving ambiguity; it can also mean accepting weak support and undefined boundaries. Ask what the team wants to preserve, what it wants to change, and who has authority to change it. A founding employee can gain unusual scope, but should not assume influence without a sponsor. “You will define the culture” is a promise that needs resources and decision rights." },
      { heading: "The cost of sameness", body: "When every hire shares the same background and communication habits, a team may move quickly because less explanation is needed. It may also miss customer perspectives, challenge weak assumptions less often, and confuse confidence with competence. This is an argument for structured evidence, not for a quota or a guarantee about any individual. A manager can ask every candidate the same core questions, score the answers against the role, and reserve room for different but valid ways of demonstrating judgement." },
      { heading: "A polite way to test the phrase", body: "When an interviewer says “we care about culture fit,” ask: “Which two working behaviours make someone successful here, and which behaviour would make the role a poor match?” The first answer reveals the positive standard. The second shows whether the team can name a real boundary without hiding behind taste. Ask how those behaviours are assessed and what happens when an employee falls short. A thoughtful answer is not a guarantee of a healthy team, but a vague one is useful information." },
      { heading: "Do not overread a rejection", body: "A rejection using culture language can sting because it seems to judge the whole person. It may reflect a genuine mismatch, a crowded shortlist, an unstructured panel, or a manager who could not articulate the decision. Ask for actionable feedback if the organisation is willing, but do not build an identity around a phrase designed to close a process. Use the experience to sharpen your own criteria. One team’s preference is not a market verdict on your ability." },
      { heading: "What managers owe candidates", body: "Managers should define the work before defining the personality. They should state the role’s constraints, use job-relevant questions, score evidence, and record why a candidate met or missed the bar. If “culture” includes hours, travel, language, or hierarchy, say so before asking people to invest in interviews. The process cannot remove judgement, but it can make judgement inspectable. A candidate who receives a clear no may still respect the team; a candidate asked to guess the hidden rules will not." },
      { heading: "What candidates owe themselves", body: "Candidates should resist performing a fictional personality to win a role. Prepare examples of collaboration, disagreement, feedback, and a failure you can discuss without blaming others. Ask for the manager’s expectations and notice what questions are welcomed or dismissed. If the team rewards a style you cannot sustain, an offer is not necessarily good news. A mismatch discovered before joining costs less than one discovered after a relocation, a resignation, or a health crisis." },
      { heading: "The ninety-day reality check", body: "After joining, compare the interview story with the first ninety days. Are decisions made as described? Are office and meeting expectations stable? Can you disagree without social punishment? Does the manager give specific feedback, and can peers explain how work is prioritised? Keep observations factual and discuss small mismatches early. A culture is not what appears on a careers page; it is what the team repeats when a deadline, error, or disagreement makes the stated values inconvenient." },
      { heading: "Replace fit with a mutual test", body: "The better question is not whether you fit an organisation’s personality. It is whether the organisation and the candidate can work together under the role’s real conditions. That test includes skill, working habits, authority, time, and respect. It allows teams to preserve necessary standards without treating sameness as safety. It allows candidates to reject a flattering but unsustainable environment without calling themselves difficult. The phrase “culture fit” should lead to a concrete question, never end the conversation." },
    ]),
  },
  "job-hopping-stops-working-after-35": {
    sections: build([
      { heading: "The résumé that looks like a map", body: "At thirty-six, an operations leader in Bengaluru counts five employers in eight years. Each move brought a raise, a better title, or an escape from a manager who was making the work smaller. The latest interview begins differently. The panel asks what was built, who benefited, and why the candidate left before the work compounded. The problem is not a magical age boundary. It is that repeated moves become a story the candidate must explain, while the market begins paying more attention to durable scope than to the next title." },
      { heading: "There is no universal age cutoff", body: "A person can move successfully after thirty-five, and a person can become stuck after two jobs. Industry, function, level, geography, family obligations, and the health of the hiring market matter. Some roles are project-based by design; others require trust accumulated over years. Treating age as a rule hides the actual question: has each move increased the candidate’s transferable judgement, or only changed the label and compensation? A careful employer should evaluate evidence. A practical candidate should prepare that evidence anyway." },
      { heading: "The compounding argument", body: "A move resets some forms of context. A new employer must learn how you decide, colleagues must learn whether they can rely on you, and you must understand systems that may not be visible in a job description. Staying can compound trust, customer knowledge, and ownership if the organisation gives room to grow. The counterargument is that staying can also compound a narrow reputation, stagnant pay, or invisible work. Tenure is not the asset; accumulated capability and credible outcomes are." },
      { heading: "When moving was rational", body: "A résumé needs room for moves that were sensible: a project ended, a company changed direction, a role promised something materially different, a family relocation became necessary, or the candidate deliberately built a new capability. Do not invent a neat reason where the truth is messier. Explain the decision, what was learned, and why the next move was chosen. Employers do not need a confession. They need confidence that the candidate recognises patterns and will not leave every difficult phase before learning how to handle it." },
      { heading: "The raise that does not change the work", body: "A salary increase can be important, especially when a person has been underpaid or carrying family obligations. It can also conceal lateral movement. Compare each offer on fixed cash, scope, authority, manager quality, learning, location, and exit options. A job hop that buys only a new number may leave the same vulnerability intact. This is not an argument for accepting less pay. It is an argument for knowing what the extra pay is compensating you for and what capability it should help you build." },
      { heading: "The seniority tax", body: "As a career progresses, employers may expect a candidate to bring a repeatable way of solving problems, not only the ability to complete assigned work. A senior hire is often evaluated on judgement, stakeholder management, mentoring, risk, and the ability to make a system better. Frequent moves can make those dimensions harder to see if every résumé bullet describes activity rather than consequence. The answer is not to disguise tenure. It is to show the arc of ownership across roles." },
      { heading: "Indian context: notice and location", body: "Indian hiring can make a move expensive before the new job begins. Notice periods may delay joining, relocation can affect housing and family care, and the same title can involve different shift or office expectations across cities. A candidate who hops without pricing these costs may turn a nominal promotion into a cash and health setback. Ask the new employer about start-date flexibility, location, travel, and the manager’s expectations. A move is not complete when the offer is signed." },
      { heading: "The manager who wants a safe pair of hands", body: "A hiring manager may prefer someone who will stay through a difficult transformation. That preference is understandable, but it can become a coded request for loyalty without authority or fair pay. Candidates should ask what the first year is expected to contain and how success is reviewed. If the employer needs stability, it should be able to describe the work, support, and progression that make stability reasonable. Retention cannot be manufactured by suspicion alone." },
      { heading: "The counterargument for strategic mobility", body: "Some careers genuinely advance through movement. A person may need a product company to learn product judgement, a regulated environment to learn controls, or a smaller team to gain end-to-end ownership. Internal labour markets can be slow or closed, making an external move the only practical route. Strategic mobility has a through-line. Each move answers the limitation of the previous role and creates evidence for the next one. Random motion and purposeful motion can look similar in a list; the explanation must reveal the difference." },
      { heading: "A clean way to tell the story", body: "Use a three-part account for each transition: the condition at entry, the work that changed, and the reason to move. “I joined to own a service, reduced repeated incidents, then moved because the next scope did not exist” is stronger than “I wanted growth.” Keep the claims defensible and avoid blaming former employers. If a project was confidential, describe the class of constraint and the decision you made without exposing customer data. The story should show agency without pretending to control every outcome." },
      { heading: "What a portfolio can repair", body: "A portfolio is useful when a résumé’s sequence raises questions. A system note, process redesign, post-incident learning, product brief, or anonymised case can show that the candidate stayed long enough to understand a problem. It should explain the baseline, constraints, decisions, collaboration, and limitation. Do not publish employer-confidential material. A polished collection of screenshots cannot prove durable ownership. One honest case with a clear trade-off can do more." },
      { heading: "The danger of staying to look stable", body: "Fear of appearing disloyal can trap people in roles that are damaging health, narrowing skills, or violating the terms under which they joined. Long tenure does not excuse an employer from providing a workable job. Before staying, ask whether scope, pay, support, and future options are improving. If not, create an exit plan while employed. Stability is useful when it protects the ability to make a good decision, not when it turns into a reason to ignore evidence." },
      { heading: "The danger of leaving at every plateau", body: "The opposite pattern is leaving whenever novelty fades. Valuable work often enters an unglamorous middle: cleaning data, negotiating interfaces, training peers, measuring adoption, and repairing a process that nobody celebrates. A person who leaves before this stage may collect beginnings without learning how results compound. Before resigning, ask whether the problem is the role, the manager, the organisation, or discomfort with the work becoming harder. A small experiment or explicit scope conversation can distinguish them." },
      { heading: "How employers should read tenure", body: "Employers should examine the reasons and outcomes behind a sequence rather than use an age-based filter. A candidate with short roles may have handled acquisitions, contracts, or project closures. A candidate with long tenure may have changed functions repeatedly inside one employer. Both can demonstrate durability. Structured questions about decisions, setbacks, and sustained ownership are better than a superstition about the number of months. Candidates cannot control every bias, but they can make the evidence easier to evaluate." },
      { heading: "The personal cost of another move", body: "A move changes more than a résumé. It can disrupt a child’s school, a partner’s work, elder care, commute, health routines, and social support. These costs do not make ambition less valid; they make the decision multidimensional. Write down what the move must improve and what it is allowed to worsen. If the new job offers only a title, it may not justify the transition. If it creates meaningful scope and a sustainable routine, the same move may be worth the effort." },
      { heading: "A decision test for the next offer", body: "Ask five questions: What capability will I own that I do not own now? Which person can confirm the mandate? What evidence should exist after a year? What happens if the named project changes? Can my finances and family absorb a failed fit? The answers need not be perfect. They should be specific enough to compare with staying. If the only clear benefit is a higher headline, calculate the downside of resetting trust and context before accepting." },
      { heading: "After thirty-five, the story must carry weight", body: "The market does not stop rewarding movement after a particular birthday. It becomes less patient with movement that has no visible accumulation. Senior candidates should show how their decisions reduced risk, made others effective, improved a customer or changed a system. They should also say what they still need to learn. A résumé of employers is a record of geography. A career story is a record of judgement. The latter is what makes the next move credible." },
      { heading: "A slower move can be the faster career", body: "Sometimes the strongest next step is staying long enough to finish a difficult transition, document the result, and earn a wider mandate. Sometimes it is leaving before a promised path disappears. Neither choice is virtuous in itself. The useful distinction is deliberate movement: a decision based on evidence, a tolerable downside, and a capability that travels. Job hopping stops working when movement replaces learning. It keeps working when every move makes the person more legible, more capable, and less dependent on a title." },
    ]),
  },
  "performance-review-reality-ratings-india": {
    sections: build([
      { heading: "The rating meeting has already happened", body: "In a Bengaluru team, an employee opens the calendar invite for a performance discussion and prepares to describe a difficult launch. The manager has already entered a rating into a system, or is close to doing so. The meeting may still change context, development priorities, or a factual error, but it is rarely a blank sheet. Performance reviews are part evidence, part comparison, part budget process, and part manager judgement. Understanding that mixture is more useful than believing either extreme: that ratings are purely objective or that nothing an employee does can matter." },
      { heading: "What a rating is meant to represent", body: "A rating may attempt to combine results, behaviours, scope, level expectations, and performance over a period. The exact formula differs by employer. A person can deliver a strong project and still receive a mixed assessment if collaboration, risk handling, or consistency was weak. Another person can have an ordinary quarter while demonstrating the judgement expected at a higher level. Ask what dimensions the organisation uses and which are formally documented. Do not infer a universal rule from a colleague’s review." },
      { heading: "The calibration room", body: "Many organisations compare ratings across managers before finalising them. Calibration can correct generous or harsh scoring and protect internal consistency. It can also compress nuanced work into a discussion where people who saw less of the work have more influence. Employees usually cannot observe the room, so they should make evidence portable: baseline, action, result, trade-off, and feedback from relevant partners. This is not a demand for a perfect number. It is a way to ensure the work survives the distance between delivery and decision." },
      { heading: "India’s hierarchy and visibility problem", body: "In some Indian workplaces, proximity to a manager, comfort with self-promotion, and access to senior meetings can affect what work is remembered. In other teams, written records and objective goals constrain that bias. Do not assume either pattern. Ask how cross-team contributions are collected, who can provide input, and whether remote or distributed employees are assessed through the same evidence. A quiet contributor should not have to become theatrical to be legible, but they do need a record others can verify." },
      { heading: "The goal was not the whole job", body: "A goal sheet often captures planned output while the year produces incidents, changed priorities, urgent customer work, and maintenance that nobody forecast. Managers should explain how changed context is considered. Employees should document why a goal moved, what alternative work was taken on, and what result followed. “The priority changed” is not automatically an excuse or a failure. It is a fact that needs a decision trail. Review quality depends on whether the organisation can evaluate adaptation as well as completion." },
      { heading: "Why impact and effort diverge", body: "A person can work late on a task that changes little, while a short design decision prevents months of rework. Reviews become distorted when effort is the only visible currency. Describe the mechanism of impact: what risk was avoided, what decision became possible, what customer or colleague was unblocked, or what recurring cost was reduced. Do not manufacture a number where none exists. A qualitative result can be credible when the chain from action to consequence is clear." },
      { heading: "The feedback that arrives too late", body: "A review should not be the first time an employee hears that their communication, prioritisation, or technical depth was below expectations. Reality is less tidy. Managers sometimes postpone difficult feedback, and employees sometimes discount signals that are uncomfortable. Ask for examples, not adjectives. A useful feedback statement names the situation, behaviour, consequence, and expected alternative. If the manager cannot provide an example, ask what evidence would demonstrate improvement rather than arguing about a label." },
      { heading: "When a rating affects money", body: "Increments, bonuses, promotions, and performance improvement processes may be connected to ratings, but the connection varies by company and cycle. Treat compensation language as a document question. Ask what is guaranteed, what depends on budget or target achievement, and when an outcome is final. A rating discussion is not the place to make legal conclusions about pay or discrimination. If a material employment consequence is disputed, review the policy and seek qualified professional guidance rather than relying on a colleague’s precedent." },
      { heading: "The employee’s evidence file", body: "Keep a lightweight record during the year: goals, changed priorities, shipped work, incidents, decisions, feedback, and dependencies. Include what did not work and what you changed. The file should be lawful to retain and should not contain customer secrets, source code, or another person’s private information. Its purpose is recall, not litigation theatre. At review time, send a concise summary aligned to the employer’s dimensions. Make it easy for a manager to verify the claim." },
      { heading: "The manager’s evidence problem", body: "Managers often inherit teams, priorities, and people they did not see directly. They may rely on status reports or the loudest stakeholder. A good manager asks for multiple views, distinguishes contribution from visibility, and records decisions before memory becomes political. They also explain the limits of a rating. An employee deserves to know whether the score reflects results, level readiness, behaviour, budget constraints, or a combination. “That is just calibration” is not enough context." },
      { heading: "The promotion conversation hiding inside the review", body: "A strong rating does not automatically equal promotion. Promotions may depend on sustained scope, an available level, organisational design, or evidence beyond one cycle. Conversely, a person can be operating at a higher level while the process is slow. Ask which behaviours or outcomes are missing, who decides, and when the evidence will be reviewed. If the answer is only “keep doing good work,” request a concrete example and a checkpoint. Scope without a route to recognition is a risk worth naming." },
      { heading: "A rating can be fair and still disappointing", body: "Fairness does not mean receiving the outcome you wanted. A manager may compare the work against a level standard and conclude that the result was solid but not exceptional. The employee can disagree with the weighting and still ask what would change the assessment. The reverse also matters: a flattering rating can hide that the role has stopped expanding. Treat the score as one signal about the organisation’s evaluation system, not as a complete measure of ability." },
      { heading: "The appeal or correction route", body: "Some employers provide a review, appeal, skip-level, or HR channel; others do not. Ask what the policy says, what can be corrected, and by when. A factual error is easier to raise than a disagreement with judgement: a missing project, wrong date, or misattributed decision should be separated from a debate about quality. Keep the tone precise. If the process raises concerns about retaliation, discrimination, or contract terms, consult the relevant policy and an appropriate qualified adviser rather than making a public accusation." },
      { heading: "Remote work and the performance record", body: "Distributed teams can make invisible work especially vulnerable: mentoring, incident response, documentation, and coordination happen outside the meeting where a manager forms an impression. The solution is not constant self-broadcasting. Use regular written updates that connect activity to outcome and identify a decision or dependency. Ask how asynchronous work is considered. A manager should not reward online presence as a substitute for results, and an employee should not assume that good work is automatically visible." },
      { heading: "What to do after a low rating", body: "First separate the score from the facts. Read the review, list each claim, mark what is specific, and identify which examples you accept or need to correct. Then ask for two or three priorities, the support available, and a date for checking progress. Do not promise a personality transformation. Agree on observable changes in work. If the process becomes a formal performance plan, read its terms carefully and ask qualified help about any contractual or legal concern. Keep communication factual and timely." },
      { heading: "What to do after a high rating", body: "A high rating is a moment to ask what responsibility can follow. Which decisions can you own, which problems are now yours, and what evidence would support the next level? Also ask how compensation and promotion decisions are separated. A strong review can become a trap if it rewards the same narrow work indefinitely. Use the recognition to negotiate scope, sponsorship, or a documented path rather than assuming the organisation will infer your ambition." },
      { heading: "A better review conversation", body: "Bring a one-page account: the commitments, changes, outcomes, limitations, and next-scope request. Ask the manager to distinguish results, behaviours, level, and organisational constraints. Listen for examples and write down the follow-up. If disagreement remains, state it without pretending it is resolved. The goal is not to make a rating feel pleasant. It is to make the reasoning inspectable and the next opportunity concrete. That is what turns a ritual into a useful operating conversation." },
      { heading: "The number is a signal, not a biography", body: "Performance ratings carry consequences, but they are made inside a particular manager relationship, business cycle, calibration process, and evidence set. They can reveal how the organisation values work and where it believes a person is ready to grow. They cannot define a career in isolation. Keep the record, ask for the mechanism, correct the facts, and decide whether the environment can recognise the work you want to do. A review should inform the next decision, not become the only story about your capability." },
    ]),
  },
  "tech-lead-trap-responsibility-authority": {
    sections: build([
      { heading: "The title arrives with a longer meeting list", body: "A developer in Pune becomes tech lead after a release goes well. The new title appears in a chat announcement; the old sprint work remains on the board. Suddenly the developer is expected to review designs, answer incidents, mentor two colleagues, explain trade-offs to product, and still finish the tickets that made the promotion feel deserved. This is the tech lead trap when responsibility expands faster than authority. The title can be a valuable apprenticeship, but only if the organisation decides what the lead may change, stop, prioritise, and delegate." },
      { heading: "Technical leadership is not senior ticket delivery", body: "A senior developer can be excellent at solving hard problems and still need a different practice as a lead. The lead makes context available, frames trade-offs, sequences risk, and helps a group make decisions it can defend. That work may produce fewer visible commits and more durable system behaviour. The counterargument is that a lead who stops understanding implementation can become a coordinator detached from reality. The role needs enough technical depth to challenge assumptions, but not an expectation that one person remains the fastest individual contributor and the team’s operating system." },
      { heading: "Name the authority before accepting it", body: "Ask which decisions the lead owns: design approval, technical priorities, incident command, standards, staffing input, vendor choice, or release readiness. A lead may recommend without deciding, or decide within a boundary that a principal engineer or product manager can override. Neither arrangement is wrong if it is explicit. Responsibility without a lever is a predictable source of blame. Write down the decision rights and escalation route before the title becomes an informal promise." },
      { heading: "The product boundary", body: "A tech lead is often accountable for technical consequences while product controls priority and scope. That division can work when both sides agree on risk, sequencing, and the conditions for a release. It fails when product promises a date and the lead is expected to absorb every compromise silently. Ask who can negotiate scope, who accepts residual risk, and how a disagreement is recorded. Technical leadership is not a veto over product. It is also not a licence for product urgency to erase engineering evidence." },
      { heading: "The incident at 2 a.m.", body: "During an outage, the lead is called because people trust their judgement. Afterward, the lead is asked why detection was late, why the rollback was difficult, and why the team had not rehearsed the path. Those are fair questions only if the lead could influence monitoring, runbooks, staffing, and change controls. Incident ownership should include authority to improve the conditions that produced the incident. Otherwise the title turns accountability into a personal shock absorber." },
      { heading: "India’s distributed-team complication", body: "An India-based lead may coordinate with architects, product managers, or operations leaders in Europe or North America. The team can be local while the decisions are global. Time-zone overlap, office expectations, and escalation norms shape the role more than the title does. Ask who attends the decisive meeting, which hours are protected, and whether the lead can make a call when the parent team is asleep. Global exposure is useful; global responsibility without access is a different bargain." },
      { heading: "The people work nobody budgets", body: "Code review, onboarding, coaching, interview loops, and conflict repair are often added to a lead’s week without a corresponding removal of delivery work. These tasks are not overhead; they are how a team becomes more capable. But they need capacity and recognition. Ask which commitments will be reduced, how mentoring is evaluated, and whether the lead has help from a manager or staff engineer. If every request is described as a small addition, the total role will be impossible to perform well." },
      { heading: "Architecture authority has a shape", body: "A lead does not need unilateral control of architecture to be effective. They do need a route from concern to decision. Is there a design review? Who can reject a proposal? How are exceptions documented? What happens when a dependency team refuses the interface? A mature process can distribute authority while preserving accountability. An immature one leaves the lead to persuade indefinitely and then blames them when a decision made elsewhere fails." },
      { heading: "When the title is an experiment", body: "A company may use tech lead as a trial before formal promotion. That can be fair if the experiment has a scope, sponsor, duration, and review criteria. It becomes exploitative when the person performs the role indefinitely for the promise of future recognition. Ask whether the title changes pay, level, or only expectations. If the organisation cannot commit to a promotion date, it can still commit to a written decision point and a limit on unpaid scope." },
      { heading: "The evidence of good technical leadership", body: "Keep examples that show a decision, not only an output. What constraint did the team face? Which options were considered? What risk was accepted? How did the lead make the trade-off legible, and what happened afterward? Include cases where the best leadership meant not building something. A lead’s impact may appear in fewer incidents, faster onboarding, simpler interfaces, or a team that can make good decisions without constant escalation. Do not claim causation the evidence cannot support." },
      { heading: "The counterargument: authority is earned", body: "Leads do not always receive broad authority on day one. A team may need to observe judgement before handing over a production gate or a cross-team decision. That is reasonable. The test is whether the path is visible. A manager can say: first own the service review, then lead a design decision, then evaluate the result at a named checkpoint. “Earn trust” without a defined arena asks the lead to prove themselves in an unbounded role. Trust grows faster when the experiment has edges." },
      { heading: "What to negotiate with a manager", body: "Do not negotiate only title and pay. Negotiate the work that stops, the decisions that move, the support that arrives, and the evidence that will be reviewed. A useful opening is: “If I am accountable for reliability, I need authority over the runbook and a route to change the dependency. Which existing priority should move so I can do that?” The manager may decline. That answer reveals whether the organisation wants leadership or merely wants a name attached to risk." },
      { heading: "The lead as translator", body: "Technical leadership often means translating between reliability, product value, customer impact, cost, and delivery time. The lead should not hide technical uncertainty behind jargon or reduce business pressure to ignorance. A short decision note can state the options, consequences, recommendation, owner, and review trigger. This makes disagreement safer because the group is arguing about a visible choice. It also protects the lead from being remembered as responsible for every assumption nobody wrote down." },
      { heading: "When delegation goes wrong", body: "A lead who keeps every difficult task becomes a bottleneck and teaches the team that quality depends on one person. A lead who delegates without context creates avoidable failure. Start with the decision boundary: what must be reviewed, what can be owned independently, and what signal should trigger help? Delegation is not abandoning quality. It is building a system in which more people can exercise judgement. The manager should protect time for this work rather than measuring the lead only by personal output." },
      { heading: "The performance-review mismatch", body: "A lead may be evaluated using an individual-contributor rubric while being asked to deliver team-level outcomes. That mismatch makes the role structurally unstable. Ask whether the level framework recognises influence, risk ownership, mentoring, and cross-team decisions. If the company has no separate path, clarify how these contributions will be considered. This is not legal advice about promotion or pay; it is a question about the evidence the organisation says it values. Keep the answer in writing." },
      { heading: "A thirty-day authority audit", body: "In the first month, list every responsibility assigned to the lead and the lever attached to it. Mark each as decide, recommend, execute, or escalate. Circle the items with no owner or no route to change. Review the list with the manager and product partner. This simple audit can reveal whether the trap is a misunderstanding, an overloaded design, or a deliberate expectation. It also gives the lead a professional way to discuss boundaries before an incident makes the question personal." },
      { heading: "Stay, renegotiate, or leave", body: "A tech lead role may be worth keeping when the scope is expanding, the manager protects capacity, and decisions become more local over time. Renegotiate when the work is valuable but the mandate is vague. Consider leaving when responsibility stays high, authority stays absent, the review route is fictional, and the role is harming health or credibility. There is no universal tenure to wait. Use the evidence from the authority audit and the response to your questions." },
      { heading: "The lead should not be the system’s scapegoat", body: "A good tech lead accepts accountability for decisions they can influence and makes constraints visible when they cannot. A good organisation gives the lead enough authority, time, and support to act on the risks it assigns them. The title is useful when it names a real operating role: someone who helps a team decide and learn. It is dangerous when it merely places one person between ambitious promises and the systems, budgets, and dependencies that actually determine the result." },
    ]),
  },
  "devops-sre-reality-india-oncall": {
    sections: build([
      { heading: "The pager is not a perk", body: "At 1:17 a.m. in Hyderabad, an engineer sees a production alert while the rest of the flat is asleep. The first question is not whether the engineer is committed. It is whether the alert is actionable, whether someone else is on the rota, and whether the team has authority to reduce the cause. DevOps and SRE work can provide deep systems learning and strong leverage. It can also transfer the cost of unreliable systems into interrupted sleep. The on-call tax is a design problem before it is a personal-resilience problem." },
      { heading: "DevOps is a way of working, not a tool list", body: "A job description may mention cloud, containers, infrastructure as code, observability, CI/CD, and automation. Those tools matter, but they do not reveal the operating model. Is the team building a platform, supporting application squads, or manually carrying deployments under a DevOps label? Ask what failure the role is expected to reduce, who owns production, and how changes are tested. Tool familiarity is portable; the judgement to use a tool safely is the deeper career asset." },
      { heading: "SRE’s central trade-off", body: "Site reliability work balances availability, latency, cost, delivery speed, and human capacity. A service can be made more reliable by adding redundancy, removing features, improving detection, or slowing risky change. No single number settles the trade-off. A useful team makes its service objectives, error budgets, and escalation practices visible. Where those formal terms are not used, ask how the team decides when reliability work displaces feature work. The answer tells you whether reliability is a priority or a slogan." },
      { heading: "The rota on paper and in life", body: "A one-in-several rota does not describe the burden if alerts are frequent, handoffs are poor, or the person on call is expected to work normally after a night incident. Ask how many people participate, what hours are covered, how swaps work, and what happens after a severe alert. The exact burden cannot be inferred from the schedule alone. Request a de-identified recent incident pattern or a description of a normal month, while accepting that an employer may not share confidential operational data." },
      { heading: "India’s time-zone advantage and cost", body: "An India team may provide follow-the-sun coverage for systems serving global users. That can reduce gaps, but it can also turn local engineers into the default overnight or handoff layer. “Global support” should specify which time zones, which rotations, and whether local daytime work is protected. Ask where the incident commander sits and whether the team can wake a service owner in another region. Time-zone coverage is a capability only when responsibility follows the clock." },
      { heading: "What makes an alert worth waking someone", body: "An alert should indicate a condition that needs action, not merely an interesting change in a graph. Noisy alerts train people to ignore the pager and make serious incidents harder to detect. Ask how alerts are reviewed, how thresholds are changed, and whether engineers can retire a low-value alarm. A team that celebrates heroic response while tolerating repetitive noise may be rewarding the symptom. Reliability improves when prevention and detection receive as much attention as rescue." },
      { heading: "The hidden work after the incident", body: "An incident is not finished when traffic recovers. The team must reconstruct what happened, communicate accurately, identify contributing conditions, and choose improvements. A blameless review does not mean consequence-free work; it means examining systems and decisions without using humiliation as an investigative shortcut. Ask whether action items have owners, priority, and a way to verify completion. If every postmortem ends with “be more careful,” the team is documenting disappointment rather than learning." },
      { heading: "On-call and compensation", body: "Some employers include on-call in the role; others provide allowances, time off, or informal flexibility. Do not assume a payment or a legal entitlement without checking the employment documents and applicable rules. Ask how the rota is described, what happens during a critical incident, and whether compensatory time is policy or manager discretion. This article cannot resolve contract or wage questions. It can identify the questions to take to HR or a qualified adviser before accepting a role whose real hours are unclear." },
      { heading: "The platform team’s customer", body: "A platform or SRE team may serve application engineers rather than external customers. Its success depends on adoption, safe self-service, reduced cognitive load, and reliable interfaces. A platform that merely centralises tickets can increase the burden it was meant to remove. Ask who uses the platform, what pain is being measured, and how teams give feedback. The strongest roles own a product-like problem: making a safe path easier, not becoming the path through which every request must pass." },
      { heading: "The counterargument: incidents teach fast", body: "Production responsibility can accelerate learning. Engineers see the consequences of design choices, discover system boundaries, and develop calm judgement under pressure. A bounded on-call rotation with good tooling and recovery time may be a valuable apprenticeship. The issue is not whether incidents occur; systems fail. The issue is whether the organisation learns and whether the person can sustain the schedule. Ask what the team improved after a recent incident, not whether it claims to have zero incidents." },
      { heading: "Automation is not free capacity", body: "Automating a deployment or a remediation can reduce repeated work, but it introduces maintenance, permissions, testing, and new failure modes. A team that counts only the minutes saved can create a fragile mechanism nobody understands during an outage. Ask who owns the automation, how it is tested, and what the manual fallback is. SRE judgement includes deciding not to automate when the risk or change rate makes automation unsafe. The goal is dependable leverage, not a dashboard full of completed scripts." },
      { heading: "The cloud bill in the room", body: "Reliability and cost are often discussed separately even though redundancy, retention, traffic patterns, and observability affect both. A DevOps role may be expected to reduce spend without permission to change architecture or product behaviour. Ask who owns the budget, how savings are verified, and which reliability trade-offs are acceptable. A claim of efficiency needs a baseline and a mechanism. Do not promise a percentage reduction without access to the data and the authority to change the system." },
      { heading: "Interview the escalation path", body: "Ask what happens when the on-call engineer cannot solve an incident. Who is paged next? Is the service owner available? Can the engineer roll back, disable a feature, or change capacity? Is there a clear distinction between a routine alert and a major incident? Specific answers show how the team distributes risk. A vague answer about “everyone helping” may describe solidarity, or it may mean nobody has a defined responsibility. The difference becomes visible at the worst hour." },
      { heading: "Health is an operational requirement", body: "Sleep loss, anxiety, and constant anticipatory checking are not evidence of dedication. They are costs that eventually affect judgement and safety. Candidates should ask how recovery works after overnight work and whether managers protect it when delivery pressure rises. Employees already on call can track alerts, sleep interruptions, follow-up work, and skipped recovery without exposing customer data. The record is not a complaint by itself. It is evidence for a capacity discussion and, if needed, a professional health conversation." },
      { heading: "The first ninety days", body: "Use the first weeks to map services, owners, dependencies, dashboards, runbooks, and escalation contacts. Then take one bounded reliability improvement from baseline to review. Learn whether the team can retire an alert, improve a handoff, or clarify a runbook without a long political battle. By the third month, compare the promised role with the actual rota and decision rights. Early evidence is easier to act on than a year of accumulated resentment." },
      { heading: "What a mature reliability culture looks like", body: "Maturity is not the absence of failure. It is a pattern: meaningful alerts, clear ownership, safe deployment paths, honest incident reviews, investment in toil reduction, and leaders who accept that reliability work has an opportunity cost. The team can say what it does not know. It can pause a release without treating the person who raised the concern as disloyal. These are qualitative signals, not a certification. Verify them through examples rather than slogans." },
      { heading: "Choose the tax deliberately", body: "Before accepting an on-call role, write the trade: what systems will you learn, what authority will you have, what hours are expected, and how will recovery work? Price the commute, family duties, health, and any variable compensation separately from the technical excitement. If the employer cannot explain the rota or escalation path, treat the missing information as risk. A role can be demanding and worthwhile. It becomes exploitative when the pager is predictable but the support, authority, and recovery are not." },
      { heading: "Reliability is a team property", body: "The strongest DevOps and SRE careers do not depend on one heroic engineer answering every alert. They build systems, habits, and decision paths that let more people respond safely and prevent recurrence. For the individual, that means collecting evidence of judgement: a risk made visible, a failure mode removed, a handoff improved, or an automation bounded responsibly. For the employer, it means matching accountability with authority and time. The on-call tax is worth paying only when it buys learning and a more reliable system, not when it simply hides staffing and design debt." },
    ]),
  },
};